# Intro
Two beams of light cross at the centre of a periodic box of uniform gas
(1e3 atom/cm^3, 0.1 Msun particles, 262144 particles, 51.8 smoothing lengths
on a side). The gas is nearly transparent (Z = 1e-3 Zsun). Each beam is a cubic
pulse of 10 smoothing lengths, injected into the gas that lies in the cube at
the first step, and it travels along a fixed direction. The beams meet at an
angle of 180, 90 or 60 degrees, set by `angle`.

The example shows two pulses of light that travel in different directions and
cross at the centre of the box. `angle` is the only setting that differs
between the three runs. The beam directions lie away from the borders of the
reception slots (defined under Configure), for the default frame of the slots
and 12 slots.

The pulses are injected by the test parameters
`GEARFeedback:ISRF_packet_test_beam_1` and `_2` (box corners, direction and
energy per particle in internal units; the layout of the 11 numbers is in
`examples/parameter_example.yml`). They are meant for tests, not for production runs.
`makeIC.py` writes their values to `beam_args.txt` and `run.sh` passes them
to swift. There is no star in this example.

`params.yml` fixes the time step at 9.375e-6 (internal units). Cooling is
off (`GrackleCooling:disable_cooling_for_debugging: 1`), so that the maps show
the light only. The light is deliberately slow: its speed is set by the time
step, not by c. A packet (the light that a particle hands on to its
neighbours) makes one hop of about one smoothing length per step, which is
about 20 km/s here. `GEARFeedback:ISRF_packet_light_speed` keeps its default
(`cap`, 0.99 c). The run lasts a few tens of steps.

# Configure
```
./configure --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-feedback=GEAR --with-cooling=grackle_0 --with-stars=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-tracers=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-subgrid-radiation=all --with-chealpix=path/to/chealpix --with-grackle=path/to/grackle
make -j
```
`--with-chealpix` is required by the ISRF transport. The number of reception
slots (the direction cells in which a gas particle stores the light it
receives) is the default of `--with-isrf-packet-slots` (12).

The example needs a radiation table whose `Data/Radiation` group carries
`MeanPhotonEnergyPE` and `Integrated_MeanPhotonEnergyPE`, besides the datasets
of the ISRF (section "The Data/Radiation group" of the stellar evolution
table documentation). The pychem table generator produces such a table from
its parameter file `params/chimieparam_radiation_parsec_spectral_popII.py`
(see the section "Where each Radiation field comes from" of the pychem page
"How to generate a Chemistry Table for GEAR"). The published table that
`getRadiationTable.sh` downloads predates the two datasets, so no download is
provided. Pass the file with `radiation_table=/path/to/table.hdf5 ./run.sh`;
the default is
`PopII_parsec_spectral_with_pe_photon_energy.hdf5` in this directory.

# Run
```
angle=180 ./run.sh
angle=90 ./run.sh
angle=60 ./run.sh
```

`run.sh` builds the initial conditions and runs swift in its own directory,
`run_angle<angle>`, which it empties first. Override a setting with an environment
variable: `n_threads`, `gas_density` (atom/cm^3), `gas_mass` (Msun),
`angle` (180, 90 or 60), `n_steps` (number of time steps), `swift_exe`, `run_dir`, `run_analysis`
(0 skips the plot) and `extra_args` (further swift arguments).

# Plot
`run.sh` calls `plotBeamCrossing.py` from the run directory. It reads
`geometry.json` and `snap/snapshot_*.hdf5` and writes `beam_crossing.png`:
four snapshots of the specific energy of the PE-band light (the snapshot
field `PacketSpecificEnergies`) on the gas that lies within 1.5 smoothing
lengths of the plane that holds both beams. Beam 1 runs along the horizontal
axis.
